The '''Baiduist Expulsion from Zong''' was a state-directed campaign by the [[Kingdom of Zong]] under [[Tianxiao of Zong|Emperor Tianxiao]] to eliminate the influence of [[Baiduism]] within the kingdom. The expulsion, enacted by decree of the [[Societal Assembly]] in 1606, ordered the ban of Baiduist practice within Zong, the expulsion of all non-Zong Baiduist peoples from the country, and the forced relocation of Zong Baiduists to the [[Sinchew]] colony in the [[New World]]. The campaign triggered the [[Baidu People's Rebellion]] (1606–1623), a series of informal Baiduist uprisings across the kingdom that resulted in over 100,000 deaths. By 1650, more than one million Zong Baiduists had been transported to Sinchew.

The expulsion was the culmination of over a century of escalating tension between the [[Tianism|Tianist]] establishment — the state religion of Zong, which ordained the emperor's legitimacy — and the growing Baiduist communities of the eastern coast. Modern historians have characterised the campaign as a genocide, citing the scale of the killings during the rebellion, the deliberate destruction of Baiduist institutions, and the forced displacement of an entire religious population.

== Background ==

=== Baiduism in Zong ===

{{Main|Baiduism}}

[[Baiduism]] was introduced to the [[Jade Peninsula]] through the maritime trade routes that connected the eastern Fosian seaboard to the continental interior. The religion was adopted primarily by the maritime trading communities of the Zong eastern coast, where it resonated with the seafaring culture through its emphasis on the Ocean Crossing (海渡) — the idea that the hundred crossings of Baiduist practice were like a voyage across the sea, requiring both individual effort and the grace of favorable winds. Baiduist monasteries, temples, and mercantile networks became established features of the eastern port cities, and the religion developed a distinctive Zong tradition that blended Baiduist practice with local customs.

The spread of Baiduism was uneven across the kingdom. The western interior and the capital at [[Zhongyuan]] remained predominantly [[Tianism|Tianist]], the religion of the court and the aristocratic elite. The eastern coast, by contrast, developed a mixed religious landscape in which Baiduism competed with and increasingly supplemented Tianist practice. Foreign Baiduist communities — [[Darasian people|Darasian]], [[Shansi|Shansian]], and other merchants and monks from the continental interior — maintained a visible presence in the eastern ports, serving as religious leaders, trade intermediaries, and cultural conduits between Zong and the Baiduist heartland.

=== Tianism and the imperial order ===

{{Main|Tianism}}

[[Tianism]] was the state religion of the [[Kingdom of Zong]] and the foundation of imperial legitimacy. The emperor's authority was ordained by the Tianist church order; the [[Societal Assembly]] and the court bureaucracy were staffed by Tianist elites; and the major institutions of the state — the military, the fiscal administration, and the diplomatic corps — were embedded within a Tianist framework. The religion's dominance was not merely a matter of personal belief but of political structure: to challenge Tianism was to challenge the basis of imperial rule.

The coexistence of Tianism and Baiduism had been managed, with varying degrees of success, since the religion's arrival on the peninsula. The [[Golden Age of Zong]] under [[Tianwu of Zong|Tianwu]] and [[Tianhe of Zong|Tianhe]] had been characterised by a policy of religious pluralism — tolerating Baiduism in the south and east while maintaining Tianism at court — and this accommodation had facilitated the marriage diplomacy with the [[Jyunhap]] and the [[Three Luoyan States]], whose populations included Baiduist majorities. The accommodation, however, was always contingent on Baiduism's remaining a subordinate and regional phenomenon rather than a challenge to the Tianist order.

=== The Sinchew colony ===

The Shendan Ocean expansion of the 15th and 16th centuries created the colonial infrastructure that would later be used for the Baiduist expulsion (see [[History of Zong (1390–1538)]]). The [[Sinchew]] colony, established at [[Tamsui]] in 1538, was initially a penal colony and plantation settlement — hot, disease-ridden, and humid, a place where fortunes were made or lives were lost. The colony's harsh conditions and its distance from the metropolitan centre made it a convenient destination for populations that the Zong state wished to remove from the peninsula.

== Escalation of tensions (1500s–1606) ==

The century preceding the expulsion was marked by a progressive hardening of Tianist sentiment against Baiduism. Several interrelated factors drove this escalation.

The Baiduist communities of the eastern coast had grown in wealth and influence through the Shendan trade. Baiduist merchants, who maintained commercial networks extending across the ocean to the New World and through the continental routes to Daras, commanded resources that rivalled those of the Tianist aristocracy. Their temples and monasteries, funded by mercantile wealth, became major landholders and patrons of the arts, and their communities developed a degree of institutional autonomy that the court viewed with increasing suspicion.

The foreign Baiduist presence — Darasian monks, Shansian merchants, and other continental Baiduists who resided in Zong — was a particular source of concern. These communities maintained ties to the continental Baiduist establishment and were perceived as potential agents of foreign influence. The presence of Darasian-connected religious communities within Zong was treated as a security matter as much as a religious one.

Within the Tianist establishment, a faction of hardline clerics and aristocrats argued that Baiduism was not merely a competing religion but an existential threat to the imperial order. The emperor's legitimacy was ordained by the Tianist church; if Baiduism supplanted Tianism in the eastern provinces, the theological foundation of imperial rule would be undermined. This argument gained traction in the capital as the eastern Baiduist communities grew, and as reports circulated of Baiduist influence among the merchant houses and even among lower-ranking officials.

Political clashes between Tianist and Baiduist factions occurred with increasing frequency from the mid-16th century onward. In the western provinces, Tianist officials imposed restrictions on Baiduist temple construction and monastic ordination. In the capital, the court debated measures to limit Baiduist participation in the commercial and administrative life of the kingdom. The foreign Baiduist communities were subjected to periodic surveillance, taxation, and expulsion orders, though these were inconsistently enforced.

The Baiduist usurpation of c. 1585, in which [[Foyuan of Zong|Emperor Foyuan]] of the [[House of Canglan]] seized the throne and scrapped the Tianist establishment, transformed the religious tension into an existential crisis (see [[History of Zong (1538-1606)]]). The Tianist church, which had been suppressed during Foyuan's four-year reign, returned to power after the Yuanlong restoration of 1589 with a determination to eliminate the Baiduist influence that had enabled the usurpation. Emperor Tianren, who led the restoration, resisted the hardliners' demands and pursued a policy of integration and reform that sought to reincorporate the Baiduist communities into the restored order. This policy was abandoned after the [[Canglan Rebellion]] of c. 1605, in which Baiduist conspirators assassinated Tianren and attempted to restore the Canglan dynasty. Tianren's brother and successor, [[Tianxiao of Zong|Tianxiao]], was convinced that coexistence was impossible. The expulsion decree of 1606 was the direct consequence.

== The 1606 decree ==

In 1606, [[Tianxiao of Zong|Emperor Tianxiao]] convened the [[Societal Assembly]] to address what the court described as the Baiduist crisis. The assembly, composed of Tianist nobles, senior officials, and clerical leaders, deliberated on a comprehensive response to the perceived threat.

The decree that emerged from the assembly had three components:

First, the practice of [[Baiduism]] was banned within the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. All Baiduist temples, monasteries, and religious institutions were ordered to be closed or destroyed. Baiduist worship, ordination, and proselytisation were criminalised. The Tianist church was designated as the sole legitimate religious authority within the kingdom.

Second, all non-Zong Baiduist peoples — foreign merchants, monks, and their families — were ordered to leave the country within a specified period. The decree targeted Darasian, Shansian, and other continental Baiduists who had established communities in the eastern ports. Those who failed to depart were subject to arrest and confiscation of property.

Third, all Zong nationals who practised Baiduism were to be relocated to the [[Sinchew]] colony in the [[New World]]. The decree characterised this relocation as a "spiritual redirection" — the Baiduist population would be removed from the Tianist peninsula and settled in a territory where they could practise their faith without contaminating the imperial order. In practice, the relocation was a forced deportation: Zong Baiduists were given no choice in the matter, and those who resisted were subject to military enforcement.

The decree was promulgated with the full authority of the emperor and the assembly. The [[Societal Assembly]]'s endorsement gave the expulsion the appearance of a consensual decision of the Zong political establishment, rather than the personal edict of a single ruler.

== Baidu People's Rebellion (1606–1623) ==

{{Main|Baidu People's Rebellion}}

The Baiduist population did not accept the decree passively. Within months of the promulgation, Baiduist communities across the eastern coast began to resist the enforcement of the expulsion orders. The resistance took the form of informal rebellions — local uprisings, temple defences, ambushes of enforcement officials, and the formation of Baiduist militias in the port cities.

The [[Baidu People's Rebellion]], as the series of uprisings became known, was not a unified movement. There was no central command, no coordinated strategy, and no single leader. Instead, the rebellion consisted of dozens of localised insurgencies, each responding to the specific conditions of its community. In some areas, Baiduist monks organised the defence of their monasteries with armed retainers. In others, merchant communities used their wealth to fund resistance and shelter fugitives. In the more remote coastal districts, entire communities simply refused to comply with the expulsion orders, forcing the Zong military to conduct repeated pacification campaigns.

The Zong state responded with escalating force. Military units were dispatched to the eastern provinces to enforce the decree, and the campaign that followed was characterised by its brutality. Baiduist temples were burned, monasteries were razed, and communities that resisted were subjected to collective punishment. The distinction between military enforcement and indiscriminate violence became increasingly blurred as the campaign progressed.

Over the seventeen years of the rebellion, an estimated 100,000 people were killed. The dead included Baiduist combatants, civilians caught in the fighting, and those who died during forced marches and deportations. Modern historians have characterised the campaign as a genocide, citing the deliberate targeting of a religious population, the destruction of religious institutions, and the scale of the killing relative to the Baiduist population of Zong.

The rebellion was effectively suppressed by 1623, though isolated resistance continued in remote areas for several more years. The suppression did not result from a single decisive battle but from the cumulative exhaustion of the Baiduist communities, the destruction of their institutional infrastructure, and the overwhelming military capacity of the Zong state.

== Relocation to Sinchew ==

With the rebellion suppressed, the Zong state proceeded with the systematic relocation of the Baiduist population to [[Sinchew]]. The colony, established at [[Tamsui]] in 1538, had originally served as a penal settlement and plantation colony. Its climate — hot, humid, and disease-ridden — had made it a place of hardship and high mortality, but also of opportunity for those who survived. The colony's existing infrastructure and its distance from the Zong mainland made it the designated destination for the expelled Baiduists.

The relocation was conducted over a period of approximately four decades, from the initial expulsions in 1606 through to the mid-17th century. Zong Baiduists were transported by ship from the eastern ports to Tamsui, a journey across the [[Shendan Ocean]] that took weeks and involved significant loss of life from disease, overcrowding, and shipboard conditions. Those who survived the crossing were settled in the colony, where they were expected to establish new lives under the authority of the colonial administration.

By 1650, over one million Zong Baiduists had been transported to Sinchew. The influx transformed the colony from a small penal and plantation settlement into a substantial population centre with its own Baiduist religious culture, commercial networks, and institutional traditions. The Baiduist population of Sinchew maintained a distinct Zong-influenced Baiduist practice that differed from the traditions of the continental Baiduist heartland, and the colony's growth laid the foundation for its later development as a major New World polity.

The foreign Baiduists expelled from Zong — Darasian, Shansian, and other continental communities — returned to their countries of origin or relocated to other Baiduist-majority territories. Their departure removed a significant cultural and commercial link between Zong and the continental Baiduist world, contributing to the kingdom's increasing orientation toward the Shendan Ocean and away from the continental interior.

== Consequences ==

The Baiduist Expulsion had lasting consequences for Zong, for the Baiduist religion, and for the Sinchew colony.

Within Zong, the expulsion eliminated Baiduism as a significant religious force on the peninsula. The temples and monasteries that had once dotted the eastern coast were destroyed or repurposed, and the Baiduist communities that had sustained them were dispersed. The Tianist establishment emerged from the campaign with its authority reinforced, though the violence of the suppression and the scale of the deportations left a legacy that later Zong historiography would struggle to address.

The expulsion also reshaped Zong's relationship with the continental Baiduist world. The removal of the foreign Baiduist communities severed commercial and religious ties between Zong and the Darasian and Shansian interior, accelerating the kingdom's eastward orientation toward the Shendan Ocean and the New World. In this sense, the expulsion was both a religious and a geopolitical act: it removed a population that was perceived as a continental fifth column and redirected Zong's external relationships toward the oceanic sphere.

For [[Sinchew]], the expulsion was foundational. The Baiduist population transported from Zong became the demographic base of the colony, and their religious and cultural traditions shaped the colony's development. The Zong Baiduist tradition — with its emphasis on the Ocean Crossing and its maritime devotional practices — proved well suited to the colonial environment, and the Baiduist institutions established in Sinchew became centres of learning, commerce, and community organisation that endured long after the expulsion itself.

